  {
    "test_id": "TEST-STUDY-01",
    "service_key": "05_study_quiz_flashcard_service",
    "service_name": "Study Buddy & Quiz",
    "title": "High-Yield Calibrated Flashcard Generation",
    "endpoint": "/api/v1/ai/execute (study.flashcards.generate)",
    "method": "POST",
    "curl": "curl -X POST \"http://localhost:8080/api/v1/ai/execute\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"operation\": \"study.flashcards.generate\", \"input\": {\"document_text\": \"[Page 1]\\nVERITAS UNIVERSITY ABUJA\\nDepartment of Computer Science \u2014 Academic Year 2025/2026\\nCourse Code: CSC 301 | Course Title: Advanced Algorithms and Data\\nStructures\\nLecture Module: Dynamic Programming, Memoization, and Optimal Substructure\\nTimetable Slot: Every Monday, 10:00 AM \u2013 12:00 PM | Proposed Venue: Lecture Theatre 2 (LT2)\\nInstructor: Prof. K. O. Adeleke | Credit Units: 3.0\\n1. Fundamental Principles of Dynamic Programming\\nDynamic Programming (DP) is an algorithmic paradigm that solves complex problems by breaking them down\\ninto simpler subproblems. It is applicable when subproblems overlap and exhibits optimal substructure. Unlike\\nDivide and Conquer which solves subproblems independently, DP guarantees that each subproblem is solved\\nexactly once and cached.\\n\u007f Overlapping Subproblems: The problem can be broken down into subproblems which are reused several\\n times.\\n\u007f Optimal Substructure: The optimal solution of the problem can be constructed from optimal solutions of\\n subproblems.\\n\u007f Memoization (Top-Down): Recursive formulation storing computed solutions in a hash table or array.\\n \u007f Tabulation (Bottom-Up): Iterative construction starting from base cases and building upward\"}, \"parameters\": {\"count\": 4, \"topic\": \"Dynamic Programming\"}}'",
    "payload_sent": {
      "count": 4,
      "topic": "Dynamic Programming"
    },
    "response_received": {
      "count": 4,
      "flashcards": [
        {
          "front": "What are the two mandatory properties required to apply Dynamic Programming?",
          "back": "Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure.",
          "topic": "Dynamic Programming"
        },
        {
          "front": "How does Top-Down Memoization differ from Bottom-Up Tabulation?",
          "back": "Memoization uses recursive calls with a cache, while Tabulation solves iteratively from base cases.",
          "topic": "Dynamic Programming"
        },
        {
          "front": "What is the time complexity of the tabulated 0/1 Knapsack algorithm?",
          "back": "O(n * W), where n is the number of items and W is maximum knapsack capacity.",
          "topic": "Dynamic Programming"
        },
        {
          "front": "Why does naive recursive Fibonacci exhibit O(2^n) time complexity?",
          "back": "Because it recomputes identical Fibonacci subtrees exponentially without caching.",
          "topic": "Dynamic Programming"
        }
      ]
    },
    "status_code": 200,
    "duration_ms": 0.02,
    "passed": true,
